  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remaining dry with above normal temperatures, \nthen some rain possible late this week into early next week.\n\nCurrent satellite as of 3 PM shows a mid-level stratus deck in\nplace across the bulk of the forecast area, but there are some\nbreaks in spots. Temperatures have warmed into the low to mid\n60s where the cloud cover is thicker, and upper 60s to low 70s\nwhere there have been more breaks of sun. Winds this afternoon are\ngenerally light out of the south. Clouds are expected to trend\ndownward in coverage slightly overnight, but there will still \nbe passing clouds here and there. Overnight lows tonight are \nforecast to be in the 40s.\n\nUpper ridging will build overhead tomorrow as high pressure\nremains in place offshore. Dry conditions and above normal\ntemperatures are forecast beneath a mixture of clouds and sun.\nHigh temperatures will generally be in the lower to middle 70s,\nand winds will remain light out of the south. Patchy fog may be\npossible tomorrow night, with lows in the upper 40s to lower\n50s.\n\nDry conditions and above normal temperatures will persist\nthrough the daylight hours on Wednesday. High temperatures will\nonce again reach well into the 70s, and winds will begin to pick\nup slightly out of the south. Mostly sunny skies are expected to\nstart the day, but high clouds will be on the increase during\nthe afternoon and evening hours in advance of an upper trough\napproaching from the west. A shortwave disturbance embedded\nwithin the broader trough will lift northeastward through the\nGreat Lakes Sunday night. This system has trended further \nnorthwest and a bit further away from the area, but a few \nshowers could still be possible by later Wednesday night.\n\nThe synoptic pattern across the eastern CONUS becomes much more \nactive at the end of this week, through the weekend, and into early \nnext week. A series of large upper troughs over the Midwest are \nforecast to steadily push north and east toward the Mid-Atlantic. \nOne notable change over the last several model cycles is that the \ninitial upper trough does not pass directly through our area, \ninstead slides northeast across the OH Valley into PA. A weaker \nshortwave ahead of the main trough does cross our region Thursday, \nand this likely brings at least scattered showers and a few \nthunderstorms. However, given that the strongest upper level support \nnow remains north of our area, we probably won't be getting a lot of \nprecipitation like earlier models run depicted. Above normal \ntemperatures likely to end the week as highs reach the upper 60s to \nmid 70s. More abundant cloud cover on Friday keeps temps a few \ndegrees lower than Thursday.\n\nThe secondary upper trough and an associated cold front dig into the \nMid-Atlantic Friday afternoon into Friday night. However, it appears \nthis second system will be rather progressive and models indicate \nthe surface reflection \"skipping\" the Alleghenies, then redeveloping \neast of I-95. If that occurs we'll see little precip outside of the \nmountains. Another hindering signal is models indicate a potential \ndry slot ahead of the cold front, which also cuts down on how much \nshower activity can develop. \n\nMost of the mid-level vorticity associated with this second upper \ntrough cuts off over the central Great Lakes Friday night into \nSaturday, then pushes well to our north/east Sunday. Given the \nrecent trends in the evolution of the synoptic pattern late this \nweek, the NBM PoPs are likely way too high for Thursday and Friday. \n\nLooking ahead at Sunday into early next week, the active pattern \nbrings another strong trough to the region Sunday night into Monday. \nThis system is forecast to be much larger than the previous two, and \nbring colder air in its wake. Periods of showers are possible at \ntimes on Monday, though model timing varies so this could change in \nthe coming days.",
